    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for those examples. One thing my doctor mentioned was \"glycemic load\" in addition to the index. Can you explain how glycemic load differs from the glycemic index, and why the distinction is important when planning meals?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Glycemic Index (GI) measures how quickly a specific food raises blood sugar, but Glycemic Load (GL) takes into account both the speed *and* the actual amount of carbohydrates eaten. GL is considered a more practical measure because it factors in portion size; a small amount of high-GI food might have a low GL. Therefore, prioritizing low GL foods helps ensure that even if a food category has a moderate GI, the actual impact on your blood sugar from a typical serving remains manageable.",
      "source": "target",
